179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The concept of 'sustainability' has become a buzzword in recent years, yet its core meaning is often misunderstood. At its heart, sustainability is about meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. It is built on three pillars: environmental protection, social equity, and economic viability. Too often, discussions about sustainability focus solely on the environmental aspect, such as reducing carbon footprints or conserving natural resources. While crucial, this perspective is incomplete. True sustainability also requires creating fair and equitable societies where all individuals have access to opportunities and a good quality of life. Furthermore, a sustainable model must be economically viable; businesses and economies must be able to thrive without depleting resources or causing social harm. A truly sustainable solution is one that finds a harmonious balance among these three pillars.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
